# Risk Register — Planning
#
# PMBOK Guide 6th ed., 11.2 Identify Risks, 11.3 Perform Qualitative Risk
# Analysis, 11.5 Plan Risk Responses.

screen: risks
title: Risk Register
phase: Planning
process: 11.2 Identify Risks / 11.5 Plan Risk Responses
order: 60
requires: charter
teaches: |
  How to write a risk so that someone can act on it, how probability and
  impact rank a register, and what the five response strategies actually mean.
  By the end you should be able to explain the difference between a risk and an
  issue without hesitating.
before: |
  The charter's overall risk posture. You can identify risks at any point,
  and should; this screen is where they get owners and planned responses.
after: |
  Everything you watch for the rest of the project. A risk with an owner and
  a response is managed; one without is a worry you wrote down.
intro: |
  A risk is an uncertain event that would affect the project if it happened. It
  is not a problem: a problem has already happened, and belongs on the Issue
  Log.

  Risks are scored by probability and impact, and each one gets an owner and a
  planned response. A register without owners and responses is a worry list.
source: |
  PMBOK Guide, 6th ed., Project Risk Management: 11.2 Identify Risks (output:
  the risk register), 11.3 Perform Qualitative Risk Analysis (probability and
  impact assessment) and 11.5 Plan Risk Responses.

field register
  prompt: What might happen that would affect this project? Write cause, event and effect.
  check rows>=3: Three risks is the floor for a register worth reviewing. A project with no risks identified is a project nobody has thought about hard yet.
  check column:owner: A risk with no owner is a worry. Name the person who watches it.
  check column:response: A risk with no planned response is a risk you have accepted without saying so.
  label: Risks
  type: table
  origin: standard
  view: matrix
  title_column: risk
  rows_column: probability
  cols_column: impact
  tone_column: status
  source: |
    Risk register (11.2 output), scored in 11.3 Perform Qualitative Risk
    Analysis and given responses in 11.5 Plan Risk Responses.
  why: |
    Probability times impact is what ranks the register, so attention goes to
    the risks that matter rather than the ones most recently mentioned.

    The five response strategies for threats are: avoid (remove the cause),
    transfer (make it someone else's exposure), mitigate (reduce probability or
    impact), accept (do nothing, knowingly), and escalate (it is outside the
    project's authority). Opportunities get the mirror set: exploit, share,
    enhance, accept, escalate.

    A good risk statement has a cause, an event and an effect: "because X, Y
    may happen, which would lead to Z."
  if_blank: |
    Risks are discovered as problems, at the point where the only option left
    is to react.
  example: |
    R5 | One late review gate means defects surface at the most expensive moment | Schedule | High | High | Mitigate | Review after M4, inside the slack | You | Open
  column ref
    label: ID
    type: line
    width: 1
  column risk
    label: Risk (cause, event, effect)
    type: text
    width: 5
  column category
    label: Category
    type: choice
    options: Schedule, Scope, Technical, External, Quality, Resource
    width: 2
  column probability
    label: Probability
    type: choice
    options: High=danger, Medium=warn, Low=faint
    width: 2
  column impact
    label: Impact
    type: choice
    options: High=danger, Medium=warn, Low=faint
    width: 2
  column response_type
    label: Strategy
    type: choice
    options: Avoid=ok, Transfer=neutral, Mitigate=accent, Accept=faint, Escalate=warn
    width: 2
  column response
    label: Planned response
    type: text
    width: 5
  column owner
    label: Owner
    type: line
    width: 2
  column status
    label: Status
    type: choice
    options: Open=accent, Watching=neutral, Closed=ok, Occurred=danger
    width: 2

field thresholds
  prompt: How much risk is acceptable before you have to act?
  check filled: Without a threshold, every risk is argued from scratch and the loudest concern wins.
  label: Risk thresholds and appetite
  type: text
  origin: standard
  source: |
    11.1 Plan Risk Management: risk thresholds, the level of risk exposure
    above which action is required.
  why: |
    It states in advance how much risk is acceptable, so the decision to act is
    not made in the moment by whoever is most worried.
  if_blank: |
    Every risk is argued from scratch, and the loudest concern wins.
  example: |
    Any risk to the October 15 closeout date is acted on. Anything that only
    costs rework is watched.
